  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Nevada federal contract obligations are $3B for FY2026 to date. Award activity in Nevada shows 19,950 awards so far, with an average award size of $150K. Obligations are down 25.7% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer in the state with $1.2B in obligations, followed by the Department of Energy with $1B and the General Services Administration with $513M. Facilities Support Services is the largest industry by obligations at $1B, and Engineering Services follows with $609M. Major vendors active in Nevada include MISSION SUPPORT & TEST SERVICES LLC with $953M, FLEET VEHICLE SOURCE INC with $447M, and JT4 LLC with $376M. Fiscal-year comparisons show prior-year levels that were higher: FY21 $3.1B, FY22 $3.3B, FY23 $3.7B, FY24 $4B, and FY25 $4B. Current-year totals are partial and reflect fiscal year to date activity only.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Nevada federal contracting trended recently?",
        "body": "Obligations are down 25.7% year over year. Nevada obligated $3B in FY2026 to date, after annual totals of $4B in FY25 and $4B in FY24. The drop accompanies shifts in the largest buying agencies, where the Department of Defense and the Department of Energy together account for a majority of current obligations. Facilities Support Services and Engineering Services remain the top industries by obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers and vendors in Nevada now?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the top buyer with $1.2B, followed by the Department of Energy with $1B and the General Services Administration with $513M. The largest vendors are MISSION SUPPORT & TEST SERVICES LLC with $953M, FLEET VEHICLE SOURCE INC with $447M, and JT4 LLC with $376M. Facilities Support Services leads industries at $1B in obligations, and Automobile Manufacturing shows $447M in obligations."
      }
    ]
  },
